          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Are there eating habits that tend to make reflux worse?",
          "answer": "Several eating habits are commonly linked with worse reflux, though the effect differs from person to person. The main source here is the gastroenterology educational summary \"Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers.\"\n\n**Meal size and timing.** Fatty meals slow how fast the stomach empties. This keeps food in the stomach longer, which extends the time reflux can happen. The same meal can cause more trouble as a heavy late dinner than as a midday meal. Eating late at night, and lying down soon after, are also linked with night-time symptoms. The corpus's condition summary for GERD lists meal timing and portion changes among common management steps.\n\n**Foods and drinks often named as triggers.** Coffee, chocolate, alcohol, citrus, tomato, mint, fatty or fried food, spicy food and carbonated drinks appear on most lists. Alcohol relaxes the valve between the stomach and the gullet, and carbonated drinks add gas that stretches the stomach. The evidence that cutting out all of these helps is weak, and strict avoidance is hard to keep up.\n\n**Individual triggers vary.** The usual approach is to record symptoms alongside what was eaten for a couple of weeks, then remove only the foods that repeatedly come before symptoms. The source says most people find two or three real triggers, and that these differ between people.\n\nIf your reflux is frequent, or if you have trouble swallowing, weight loss, vomiting blood, or black stools, speak with a clinician promptly. Those symptoms need assessment and are not something to manage by diet alone. A clinician or registered dietitian can help you work out your own triggers.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
